 ## Background
-The use of geometric constraints in neural radiance fields emerged to address floaters and noisy geometry in sparse-view reconstruction [13295-normal-consistency-and-alignment.md]. Early methods relied on photometric loss, leading to ambiguous geometry, prompting the introduction of curvature regularization [2911-curvature-loss.md] and normal consistency losses [13295-normal-consistency-and-alignment.md]. Subsequent works extended these principles to material fields [7299-material-smoothness-loss.md] and style transfer contexts [14883-geometry-consistency-loss.md]. Specific variants target architectural surfaces [S2], vector fields [S4, unofficial], and surfel rendering [S5, unofficial], while alignment-aware training addresses spatial misalignment between rendered and ground truth images [S1] and feature-space alignment mitigates photo-consistency violations [S6, unofficial].
+The use of geometric constraints in neural radiance fields emerged to address floaters and noisy geometry in sparse-view reconstruction [G]. Early methods relied on photometric loss, leading to ambiguous geometry, prompting the introduction of curvature regularization and normal consistency losses [G]. Subsequent works extended these principles to material fields and style transfer contexts [G]. Specific variants target architectural surfaces [S2], vector fields [S4, unofficial], and surfel rendering [S5, unofficial], while alignment-aware training addresses spatial misalignment between rendered and ground truth images [S1] and feature-space alignment mitigates photo-consistency violations [S6, unofficial].
 
